                    "Asked whether generated video will overwhelm creators, Rober said he is not worried, and then described where his own team draws the line.  <mark>Generative AI is a thought partner on writing and on some thumbnail work</mark>.  He thinks it could let him scale himself in certain ways.  And he expects to use it in Class CrunchLabs, his schools programme for third to eighth graders, to adapt lesson material to individual students, including those who struggle with reading.  That programme has a <mark>2.5 million dollar grant from Google.org</mark>.",
                    "Where it does not go is the capture.  His argument is that in science you actually want to see the lava melt through the object, and that viewers knowing it is real is what makes it remarkable.  He adds that it remains to be seen what the public's appetite is for purely generated content.",
                    "As a rule that is cleaner than most of what gets written about this: AI goes <mark>upstream of the camera</mark>, into idea development, scripting, and title and thumbnail testing, and stays <mark>out of anything that works as evidence</mark>.  On a channel with <mark>85 million subscribers</mark> whose whole proposition is demonstration, the footage is not content.  It is a receipt.",
                    "The cost is the part people skip.  Keeping generation out of the capture means you still pay for the shoot, the failed takes and the day the thing does not melt.  The saving lands in the places nobody films: the script passes, the thumbnail variants, the lesson differentiation.  That is a reasonable trade, and it is the opposite of how most brands are currently being sold these tools.",
